---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const project = {
  title: "Wearable Activity Monitoring from IMU Signals",
  tag: "mHealth",
};
---

<Layout title={project.title}>
  <a href={base} class="back">← Back to projects</a>
  <article>
    <span class="tag">{project.tag}</span>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>

    <p class="summary">
      A four-task inference pipeline over 676 real-world IMU recordings from a smartwatch
      and paired smartphone, collected along five urban commute routes in Zurich. The system
      predicts which route was taken, where on the body the watch was worn, which activities
      occurred, and how many steps were taken — all from raw sensor streams, with no GPS
      used at inference time. Activity detection achieves cross-validated accuracy of 99.7%
      for running and 95.2% for standing; a custom step counter eliminates vibration-induced
      false counts through a physiological minimum threshold.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        This was a Kaggle competition run as part of ETH Zürich's Mobile Health and Activity
        Monitoring course. Each recording captured a single commute: a participant walked,
        ran, cycled, or stood between two fixed points while wearing a smartwatch at one of
        three body positions — left wrist, belt, or right ankle — with a phone in their
        pocket or bag logging additional sensors. The task was to infer four things
        simultaneously from those raw signals: the route taken, the watch placement, which
        activities occurred, and the total step count.
      </p>
      <p>
        The labels were deliberately incomplete. Activity labels recorded only <em>whether</em>
        an activity happened during a recording, not when or for how long. Step counts were
        verified by hand counter for a small subset and unavailable for the rest. This meant
        the approach had to handle noisy supervision and make careful decisions about what
        the labels could and couldn't be trusted to say.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Signal processing foundation</h2>
      <p>
        All four sub-tasks share a common pre-processing layer: bandpass filtering to isolate
        motion-frequency content (0.5–8 Hz for movement signals, lower cutoffs for orientation),
        missing value replacement, and detrending. A shared feature library then computes
        statistics — mean, RMS, percentiles, IQR — plus spectral band powers and peak rate
        for any signal segment. Building this once and reusing it across all four tasks kept
        the codebase consistent and made it much faster to debug when something went wrong
        downstream.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Route classification</h2>
      <p>
        The five routes between Zurich Hauptbahnhof and ETH differ mainly in elevation:
        three climb, two descend. Barometric pressure — converted to an approximate altitude
        offset — proved to be the strongest discriminator. Rather than using only the net
        elevation change, the features also capture the <em>shape</em> of how altitude
        evolves over the first 15%, 30%, and 50% of each recording. This distinction matters
        because two routes share a similar total gain but differ in whether the climb is
        concentrated at the start or spread across the full journey. Magnetometer readings
        add orientation cues, and a temporal histogram encodes how signals drift over the
        full recording. An Extra Trees classifier trained on these features assigns each
        recording to one of the five routes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Watch placement classification</h2>
      <p>
        Where the watch is worn fundamentally changes what the sensors record. Ankle
        placement produces sharp, high-amplitude impacts at each footstrike; wrist placement
        gives smoother arm-swing oscillations; belt placement dampens both. A naive approach
        — extracting features from the full recording — would average over all the activity
        mix and blur these placement-specific signatures. Instead, the classifier operates on
        20-second sliding windows and trains on window-level features. At inference, predicted
        probabilities are averaged across all windows of a recording, and the highest average
        wins. This soft aggregation is more reliable than a majority vote when one window
        happens to contain an atypical motion segment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Activity recognition under weak supervision</h2>
      <p>
        Activity labels in this dataset are what the literature calls weak or soft labels:
        a recording marked "walking" and "running" tells you both happened at some point, but
        gives no information about when, in what order, or for how long. A participant who
        sprinted for two minutes and walked for twenty carries the same label as one who ran
        the entire route. This makes the standard supervised learning setup difficult — if
        you train a model on the full recording as a single example, you're treating 20
        minutes of ambiguous signal as uniformly representative of every labelled activity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The solution was to break each recording into 60-second windows with a 10-second
        stride, extract features from each window independently, and train one binary
        classifier per activity. A window from a running-positive recording doesn't
        necessarily contain running — but by taking the <em>maximum predicted probability
        across all windows</em>, the approach asks: did any 60-second segment in this
        recording look like this activity? That question is much better matched to what the
        label actually says. The decision threshold for each classifier is then tuned
        separately via 5-fold cross-validation rather than fixed at 0.5, because class
        imbalance and the weak-label structure push the natural operating point away from
        the naive default.
      </p>
      <p>
        For activities with a clear kinematic signature, this worked extremely well. Running
        reached a cross-validated adjusted balanced accuracy of 99.7% (threshold 0.15) and
        cycling 97.1% (threshold 0.17) — their spectral fingerprints are distinctive enough
        that even a low model confidence is reliable evidence. Walking was harder at 90.0%
        (threshold 0.78): slow walking produces signals that overlap with gentle standing
        movement, and the high threshold was needed to suppress false positives from
        low-activity segments in other recordings.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Standing detection: working around weak labels</h2>
      <p>
        Standing posed a deeper version of the same problem. Unlike locomotion, stillness
        has no positive kinematic signature — it's the absence of rhythmic movement. A
        recording labelled "standing" might contain one quiet minute embedded within 20
        minutes of energetic walking. Taking the maximum window probability works for
        running because running windows are distinctive; for standing, the relevant windows
        look like the quietest moments in any recording, making them nearly indistinguishable
        from noise floors in non-standing recordings.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix was a pseudo-label selection step. For each standing-positive recording, a
        composite quietness score is computed per window — the average percentile rank of
        signal RMS, 95th-percentile amplitude, and spectral power in the motion band. The
        single quietest window is selected as the training positive, under the assumption
        that the genuine standing period produced the calmest signal in the recording. For
        standing-negative recordings, the noisiest quarter of windows becomes the training
        negatives, since those are confidently locomotion. This asymmetric selection
        concentrates the classifier's attention on the true boundary between stillness and
        movement, and lifted accuracy from around 71% to 95.2%.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Step counting</h2>
      <p>
        The step counter processes 10-second windows of filtered accelerometer magnitude and
        combines two independent estimates: a peak detector that counts footfall candidates
        directly, and an autocorrelation analysis that infers cadence from the signal's
        periodicity. When the two agree within a factor of two, the peak count is used; when
        they diverge — usually because a burst of noise inflated one — their geometric mean
        is taken instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two suppression gates run before any counting. A spectral check rejects cycling
        windows, where energy concentrates in a low-frequency pedalling band with little
        higher-frequency content. A variance threshold rejects standing-still windows. A
        separate gyroscope check catches the edge case of a handlebar-locked wrist, which
        can look like calm walking to the accelerometer alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most consequential fix came late in development. The original algorithm was
        accumulating 60–100 phantom steps on purely cycling recordings — too few to be a
        real walk, but enough to corrupt the metric. The cause was low-level road-surface
        vibration slipping through the spectral filter. Adding a physiological minimum —
        if total steps fall below 110 (roughly one minute of slow walking, the dataset's
        minimum valid activity duration), return zero — eliminated the entire class of
        false counts in one line of code.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Results</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-validated adjusted balanced accuracy: running 99.7%, cycling 97.1%, standing
        95.2%, walking 90.0%. Training all models on 396 recordings took around 22 minutes;
        inference across 280 test recordings completed in under 15 minutes on a Kaggle CPU,
        well within the 10-second-per-recording time limit imposed by the competition.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        The cycling suppression in the step counter relies on a spectral heuristic tuned to
        typical urban cadences — an unusual pedalling rate or rough cobblestones introducing
        high-frequency vibration could defeat it. The activity classifiers treat watch
        placement as irrelevant to feature extraction, but ankle-worn sensors during running
        produce very different signals from wrist-worn ones; placement-conditional models
        would likely improve walking accuracy where the ambiguity is greatest. The
        pseudo-label approach for standing also has a blind spot: a participant who stood in
        a genuinely noisy environment — on a vibrating tram platform, say — could produce a
        confounded positive that the quietness score selects incorrectly.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-decoration: none; color: #555; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #1a1a1a; }
  .tag { font-size: 0.72rem; background: #f0f4ff; color: #3b5bdb; padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 999px; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 650; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; color: #1a1a1a; }
  .summary { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #333; max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  section { max-width: 680px; }
  p { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #333; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  code { font-family: 'Menlo', 'Monaco', monospace; font-size: 0.88em; background: #f5f5f5; padding: 0.1em 0.35em; border-radius: 4px; }
</style>
